{"id": "homological-algebra-summary-heading", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "\\begin{tcolorbox}[enhanced,breakable,colback=KuangNei,frame hidden,boxrule=0pt,sharpish corners,before skip=10pt,after skip=10pt]\n\\refstepcounter{booksummary}\\label{box:homological-algebra-summary}\n\\noindent\\textbf{Box~\\thebooksummary:} {Summary of Chapter~\\ref{chap:homological-algebra} on homological algebra.}\\par", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
{"id": "summary-chain-complex-intro", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{A \\emph{chain complex} is a sequence of vector spaces and operators}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
{"id": "summary-chain-sequence", "block_type": "equation", "source_text": "\\[\n\\cdots\\to V_{k+1}\\xrightarrow{\\partial_{k+1}}V_k\\xrightarrow{\\partial_k}V_{k-1}\\to\\cdots\n\\]", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": ["formula-435dc62140b682b1"]}
{"id": "summary-chain-spaces", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{with successive operators composing to zero. It gives rise to three vector spaces at each level $k$:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item {$\\mathcal Z_k$, the null space of the boundary operator $\\partial_k$, consisting of the \\emph{cycles};}\n\\item {$\\mathcal B_k$, the range of $\\partial_{k+1}$, the \\emph{boundaries};}\n\\item {$\\mathcal H_k=\\mathcal Z_k/\\mathcal B_k$ the \\emph{homology space}.}\n\\end{itemize}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": ["formula-4231c6c1b5fad83b", "formula-2ee53cece5170822", "formula-22e71d7031a0f06a", "formula-d2997fecaea6d9bb", "formula-df73c4286b52d710", "formula-9c2f096080215afd"]}
{"id": "summary-simplicial-example", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{An important example is the \\emph{simplicial chain complex} associated to a triangulation or more general simplicial complex. The dimensions of its homology spaces are the Betti numbers of the underlying space.}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
{"id": "summary-cochain-intro", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{A \\emph{cochain complex} has the indexing reversed,}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
{"id": "summary-cochain-sequence", "block_type": "equation", "source_text": "\\[\n\\cdots\\to V^{k-1}\\xrightarrow{d^{k-1}}V^k\\xrightarrow{d^k}V^{k+1}\\to\\cdots,\n\\]", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": ["formula-60ddfadacf9d1ead"]}
{"id": "summary-cochain-objects", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{giving rise to \\emph{cocyles}, \\emph{coboundaries}, and \\emph{cohomology}.}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
{"id": "summary-duality", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{The dual spaces and dual maps of a chain complex form a cochain complex.}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
{"id": "summary-de-rham-intro", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{Another cochain complex is the \\emph{de Rham complex} which, on a three-dimensional domain, can be written}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
{"id": "summary-de-rham-sequence", "block_type": "equation", "source_text": "\\[\n0\\to C^\\infty(\\Omega)\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{grad}}C^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{curl}}C^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{div}}C^\\infty(\\Omega)\\to0.\n\\]", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": ["formula-fdf16f4062feecf7"]}
{"id": "summary-de-rham-theorem", "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "source_text": "{The de Rham map is a \\emph{cochain map} from the de Rham complex to the dual of the simplicial chain complex and, as such, induces a map on cohomology. De Rham's theorem states that the induced map is an isomorphism.}\n\\end{tcolorbox}", "source_location": {"page": 29, "bbox": [55, 54, 430, 675]}, "confidence": 0.99, "review_status": "reviewed", "formula_ids": []}
